  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "\"Eid Mubarak\" is an Arabic phrase meaning \"Blessed Eid\" or \"Blessed Feast/Festival,\" commonly used by Muslims as a traditional greeting to wish someone a joyous and blessed holiday during Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha. It signifies a blessing on the celebration and is used to express goodwill, joy, and peace. Key Usage Examples and Variations: Eid Mubarak: The standard, most common greeting, meaning \"Blessed Eid\" or \"Happy Eid\". Eid al-Fitr Mubarak: Specifically used to wish a blessed Eid at the end of Ramadan. Eid al-Adha Mubarak: Used during the festival of sacrifice. Eid Saeed: Translates to \"Happy Eid\". Kul 'am wantum bikhair: An Arabic phrase often added, meaning \"May you be well with every passing year\". Response: \"Eid Mubarak to you too!\" or \"Taqabbal Allahu minna wa minkum\" (May Allah accept from us and from you). Significance: Context: Used during the two major Islamic holidays: Eid al-Fitr (breaking the fast) and Eid al-Adha (the festival of sacrifice). When to say it: It is commonly exchanged after Eid prayers, during visits to family and friends, and in text messages or social media posts. Meaning: It acts as a blessing of fortune, prosperity, and well-being for the recipient. Synonyms and Similar Phrases: \"Blessed Festival\" \"Happy Eid\" \"Blessed Celebration\" \"Blessed Anniversary\"",
